<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Sample on Principia Materia</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/tags/sample/</link><description>Recent content in Sample on Principia Materia</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 26 Sep 2025 15:28:33 -0400</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.docsy.dev/tags/sample/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>General Information for CLI</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/general_information/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Jan 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/general_information/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;PM has a collection of command line interfaces (CLI) for performing tasks.
For the most part, a given CLI is associated with a class within PM,
though there are CLI&amp;rsquo;s that rely on multiple classes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each CLI starts with the prefix &lt;code&gt;pm-&lt;/code&gt;, followed by some descriptive word,
such as &lt;code&gt;pm-lattice&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;pm-crystal&lt;/code&gt;, etc. Assuming that the python module
argcomplete has been installed and set up, all CLI options can be seen
by typing the command name followed by &lt;code&gt;-&lt;/code&gt; and pressing tab twice.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Array Parser</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/array-parser/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Jan 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/array-parser/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;For many different tasks performed in the PM suite, a vector or matrix must be
provided at the command line or in a yaml file.
PM has an array parser which allows substantial flexibility to facilitate this
(see parse_array if interested in the details).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Consider entering the lattice vectors tag. All of the below are valid syntax.
The parser excepts delimiters: &lt;strong&gt;line break&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;semi-colon&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;comma&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;space&lt;/strong&gt;,
where line break takes highest position. If only one delimiter is present, the
result will be a vector, while two delimiters yields a matrix, etc.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Prototype Crystals</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/prototype-examples/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Jan 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/prototype-examples/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;In order to test out the code, it is useful to have sample crystals.
At present, there is a script called
&lt;em&gt;pm-prototype-xtal&lt;/em&gt; which contains such data, and the information can be seen
via tab completely &lt;em&gt;print-attribute&lt;/em&gt;. This is only meant to be temporary
until we have a more polished solution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ pm-prototype-xtal --print-attribute perovskite
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;lattice_vectors:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[[&lt;/span&gt; 3.91300000, 0.00000000, 0.00000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt; 0.00000000, 3.91300000, 0.00000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt; 0.00000000, 0.00000000, 3.91300000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;basis_atoms:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; Sr: 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[[&lt;/span&gt; 0.50000000, 0.50000000, 0.50000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; Ti: 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[[&lt;/span&gt; 0.00000000, 0.00000000, 0.00000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; O: 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[[&lt;/span&gt; 0.50000000, 0.00000000, 0.00000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt; 0.00000000, 0.50000000, 0.00000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt; 0.00000000, 0.00000000, 0.50000000&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>Symmetrizing atoms in crytal</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/pm-orbit-qrep/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Jan 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/pm-orbit-qrep/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Here we illustrate how to symmtrize displacements at a given q-point according to irreducible representations
of the little group using the script &lt;strong&gt;pm-disp-qrep&lt;/strong&gt;. Take the example of \(\bm{q}=(\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2})\)
in cubic SrTiO\(_3\). We will use &lt;strong&gt;pm-prototype-xtal&lt;/strong&gt; to generate the crystal structure file.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ pm-prototype-xtal --perovskite &amp;gt; xtal.yml
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Printing out the irreducible representations, we have&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ pm-orbit-qrep -i xtal.yml --point-group Oh --qpoint 1/2,1/2,1/2 --print-irr-reps 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Orbit Key: &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#4e9a06"&gt;&amp;#39;Sr&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;, 0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;, Atom Index: &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;A2u
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Orbit Key: &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#4e9a06"&gt;&amp;#39;Ti&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;, 0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;, Atom Index: &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;A1g
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Orbit Key: &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#4e9a06"&gt;&amp;#39;O&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;, 0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;, Atom Index: &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;0, 1, 2&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;T1u
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;We can also print out the irreducible representation vectors.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Symmetrizing displacements in Crystal</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/pm-disp-qrep/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Jan 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/pm-disp-qrep/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Here we illustrate how to symmtrize displacements at a given q-point according to irreducible representations
of the little group using the script &lt;strong&gt;pm-disp-qrep&lt;/strong&gt;. Take the example of \(\bm{q}=(\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2})\)
in cubic SrTiO\(_3\). We will use &lt;strong&gt;pm-prototype-xtal&lt;/strong&gt; to generate the crystal structure file.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ pm-prototype-xtal --perovskite &amp;gt; xtal.yml
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Printing out the irreducible representations, we have&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ pm-disp-qrep -i xtal.yml --point-group Oh --qpoint 1/2,1/2,1/2 --print-irr-reps
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#4e9a06"&gt;&amp;#39;Sr&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;, 0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;T2g
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#4e9a06"&gt;&amp;#39;Ti&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;, 0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;1&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;T1u
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#4e9a06"&gt;&amp;#39;O&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;, 0&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#0000cf;font-weight:bold"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#0000cf;font-weight:bold"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; 4&lt;span style="color:#ce5c00;font-weight:bold"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;A1g+Eg+T1g+T2g
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;We can also print out the irreducible representation vectors.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Crystal structure</title><link>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/pm-crystal/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Jan 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://example.docsy.dev/docs/functionality/pm-crystal/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Interfaces to Crystal class.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ pm-prototype-xtal --print-attribute perovskite &lt;span style="color:#000;font-weight:bold"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; pm-crystal -i -
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>